(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"An 84-year-old woman spent 9 days in the hospital for a large pericardial effusion that needed two procedures. She is now weak and needs help to walk. Besides cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery, which service is most appropriate before she goes home?","choices":["Rehabilitation","Infectious disease","Genetics","Nephrology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rehabilitation is listed alongside cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery in the care of older adults with pericardial disease. At 84, weak and needing help to walk after 9 days and two procedures, she fits that need, and older adults also carry higher morbidity and mortality with surgical interventions. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which surgery treats aortic dissection?","choices":["Ultrasound-guided thrombin injection","Grafting and replacing the aorta","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgery for aortic dissection grafts and replaces the diseased portion of the aorta. It follows measures to stop progression, reducing systolic pressure to 100 to 120 mmHg with esmolol or nitroprusside, and pain management, because acute untreated dissection has a high mortality rate. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man on telemetry has a prolonged QT after starting a new medication and then develops polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (QRS complexes that vary in shape and twist around the baseline). He becomes unresponsive and pulseless. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous magnesium first","Transcutaneous pacing","Defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a prolonged QT is torsades de pointes, and a hemodynamically unstable patient, here unresponsive and pulseless, is treated with defibrillation (unsynchronized cardioversion). Magnesium is the drug for torsades in a conscious patient; once he is pulseless, the shock comes first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes describes a continuous burning sensation in both feet that is the same at rest and during exercise. Her pedal pulses are easily palpable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Peripheral neuropathy","Critical limb ischemia","Spinal stenosis","Peripheral artery disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral neuropathy, common in elderly patients with diabetes or alcoholism, causes a continuous burning sensation in the foot that is unaffected by exercise. Her long-standing diabetes, burning that is the same at rest and with exercise, and easily palpable pulses all fit. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a 12-second run of identical wide QRS complexes at 160 per minute on telemetry. He stays alert, with a normal blood pressure, and feels only a flutter. How should this run be classified?","choices":["Non-sustained, polymorphic","Sustained, monomorphic","Non-sustained, monomorphic","Sustained, polymorphic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three or more consecutive premature ventricular beats make ventricular tachycardia. His run lasted 12 seconds, under 30, and caused no hemodynamic collapse, so it is non-sustained, and identical complexes make it monomorphic. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy has orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat), crackles at both lung bases and diminished breath sounds at the right base. A chest radiograph is ordered. Which finding on the film would indicate pulmonary edema?","choices":["A normal film that excludes congestion","Short horizontal lines meeting the pleura","A small heart with clear lung fields","Isolated thickening of the pericardium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His orthopnea and basal crackles suggest pulmonary edema, and on the chest radiograph Kerley B lines, horizontal lines 1 to 2 cm long meeting the pleura at right angles, indicate it. The diminished breath sounds at his right base fit the pleural effusion and cardiomegaly that dilated cardiomyopathy also shows. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram changes occur in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Left bundle branch block only","Low QRS voltage in all leads","ST elevation and T wave inversion","Inferior and lateral Q waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy shows ST segment elevation and T wave inversion. Together with elevated troponin and apical wall-motion abnormalities, this mimics acute coronary syndrome, so its protocols are followed until catheterization shows normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has sudden chest pain and diaphoresis an hour after learning she has lost her savings in a financial collapse. The electrocardiogram shows ST elevation and troponin is elevated. No catheterization has been done yet. Which is the most appropriate initial approach?","choices":["Discharge home with grief counseling","Follow acute coronary syndrome protocols","Start a positive inotrope infusion","Alcohol ablation of the septum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her chest pain after losing her savings, with ST elevation and elevated troponin, may be stress cardiomyopathy, but it mimics acute coronary syndrome with no initial reliable differences. Evaluation and treatment therefore follow established acute coronary syndrome protocols, with admission to cardiology, until catheterization settles the diagnosis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable cardiomyopathy on full medical therapy says he tires after walking a block and feels anxious about exercising. He has no symptoms at rest. Which referral is most appropriate to improve his exercise tolerance?","choices":["Hospice referral for end-stage care","A competitive sports clearance clinic","Genetic screening of his children","A cardiac rehabilitation program"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His problems are limited exercise tolerance and anxiety about exercising, and cardiac rehabilitation programs aim to improve exercise tolerance and quality of life. Rehabilitative care also provides education on medication adherence, nutrition and symptom monitoring, and psychological support for coping with chronic illness. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which is the first treatment for torsades de pointes in a conscious patient?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous metoprolol","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium is given to break torsades de pointes, then more magnesium as an infusion. Amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided because they prolong the QT; if the patient becomes hemodynamically unstable, defibrillation replaces drug therapy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the most frequent presumed cause of myocarditis in developed countries?","choices":["Viral infection","Autoimmune disease","Drug reactions","Bacterial infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In developed countries, viral infection is presumed to be the most frequent cause of myocarditis, and viral myocarditis accounts for 20% of dilated cardiomyopathy cases. Named viruses include parvovirus B19, human herpesvirus 6, Coxsackie group B enteroviruses, adenoviruses, hepatitis C virus, cytomegalovirus and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV). — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with recurrent urinary tract infections has aortic valve endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Nafcillin plus rifampin","Cefazolin alone","Oral amoxicillin once","Ampicillin plus gentamicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis, and enterococcal endocarditis is treated with ampicillin plus gentamicin, both for 4-6 weeks. Listed alternatives are penicillin G plus gentamicin, vancomycin plus gentamicin, or ampicillin plus ceftriaxone, each an intravenous combination. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Erythema extending well beyond the margin of an inflamed vein raises suspicion of which condition?","choices":["Septic thrombophlebitis","Superficial phlebitis","Lipodermatosclerosis","Stasis dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erythema that extends significantly beyond the margin of the vein should raise suspicion of septic thrombophlebitis, an infection within the vein. High fever, fluctuance and purulent drainage point the same way, and septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon without a history of venous cannulation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What must follow chest or back pain in a patient with a known thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Urgent imaging","Routine yearly imaging","Reassurance and analgesia","An exercise stress test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chest or back pain in a patient with a known or suspected thoracic aortic aneurysm must undergo urgent imaging studies to rule out the aneurysm as the cause of the pain. If the aneurysm is responsible, it is symptomatic, and repair is recommended for all symptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysms. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has 3 weeks of fever and night sweats. Two separate blood cultures grow Streptococcus gallolyticus, and a transesophageal echocardiogram shows an oscillating mass on the aortic valve. He has no skin, eye or kidney findings. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Possible endocarditis","Unclassifiable for now","Endocarditis ruled out","Definite endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He meets both major criteria: positive blood cultures (Streptococcus gallolyticus in two separate sets) and echocardiographic evidence of endocardial involvement (an oscillating mass on the aortic valve). Two major criteria establish definite endocarditis, so the absent minor findings do not matter. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 19-year-old swimmer has an electrocardiogram before a sports physical. She has no symptoms. The rate is 72, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS, but the beats come closer together as she breathes in and spread apart as she breathes out. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Sick sinus syndrome","Atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A rate of 72 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS, beats closer together on inspiration and farther apart on expiration, is sinus arrhythmia. It is a normal, physiologic rhythm at 60 to 100 that is irregular with a pattern, which fits a healthy swimmer with no symptoms. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has superficial thrombophlebitis of the forearm after an intravenous catheter and asks whether the clot can travel to her lungs. Which statement about her outlook is accurate?","choices":["Pulmonary embolism is the usual outcome","Serious complications are rare","Chronic venous insufficiency follows in most","Fatal embolism is likely without treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Superficial phlebitis and thrombophlebitis rarely cause serious complications and rarely embolize, so the clot in her forearm vein is unlikely to reach her lungs. Her catheter-related case is managed with warm compresses, compression, elevation and a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID). — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and outflow tract obstruction has breathlessness and chest tightness on exertion. His blood pressure is 124/78 and heart rate is 76. He takes no cardiac medication yet. Which is the initial medication for his outflow obstruction?","choices":["A high-intensity loop diuretic","A beta blocker such as metoprolol","A nitrate such as nitroglycerin","A positive inotrope such as digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For his outflow tract obstruction with exertional breathlessness and chest tightness, a beta blocker such as metoprolol is the initial medication. As a negative chronotrope and inotrope it reduces heart rate and enhances diastolic filling by reducing ventricular stiffness and decreasing contractility; verapamil or diltiazem is the alternative. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a regular tachycardia at 170 with an identical, abnormally shaped P wave before every QRS. Adenosine briefly slows the ventricular response, but the abnormal atrial rhythm continues unchanged afterward. Which rhythm does this response point to?","choices":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Orthodromic reentrant tachycardia","Focal atrial tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia at 170 with one identical abnormal P wave before every QRS comes from a single ectopic atrial focus. Adenosine is a diagnostic tool here because it is usually less effective at terminating focal atrial tachycardia than the node-dependent reentry rhythms, so brief slowing with the atrial rhythm carrying on fits. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with no neurologic symptoms has a 90% carotid stenosis on duplex. He has advanced cancer, and his expected survival is about two years. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Endarterectomy after one more duplex","Carotid endarterectomy","Medical management","Carotid angioplasty with stenting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surgery for asymptomatic stenosis is advised only above 80% together with a life expectancy over five years. His 90% stenosis meets the first criterion, but advanced cancer limits survival to about two years, so he is managed medically with antiplatelet therapy, comorbidity control and smoking cessation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who delivered a healthy baby three months ago now has breathlessness on exertion, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and swollen ankles. She had no heart disease before pregnancy. Echocardiogram shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30%. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Peripartum cardiomyopathy","Stress cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Peripartum cardiomyopathy is a rare cause of dilated cardiomyopathy that develops in the last trimester of pregnancy or within the first 6 months postpartum. Her dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30%, three months after delivery and with no prior heart disease, fits that window. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college athlete has exertional chest pain without coronary disease and a harsh systolic murmur at the left sternal border. Hypertension and aortic valve disease have been excluded. Which echocardiogram finding confirms the clinical diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Pericardial thickening on imaging","Unexplained wall of 1.5 cm or more","Dilated thin walls with low fraction","Apical ballooning and normal septum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With hypertension and aortic valve disease excluded, the clinical diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is confirmed when echocardiography shows unexplained left ventricular wall thickness of 1.5 cm or more anywhere in the wall. The hypertrophy is most often asymmetric and septal, with a small left ventricular cavity. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man becomes hypotensive and short of breath an hour after a catheter procedure perforates his heart. Echocardiogram shows only a small pericardial effusion, estimated at about 150 mL, with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which best explains why so little fluid has compromised him?","choices":["Rapid filling of an unstretched sac","Chronic stretching of the sac","The pericardium was already calcified","The echocardiogram underestimated a liter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His effusion formed within an hour of the perforation, and acute pericardial effusions can cause hemodynamic compromise with small amounts, under 200 mL. Chronic effusions can grow large without tamponade because the pericardium stretches over time; a sudden 150 mL gives the sac no time to stretch. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man receiving chemotherapy for colon cancer comes to the emergency department with three days of left calf swelling that measures 4 cm more than the right, plus tenderness along the deep veins of the calf. He has no history of clots and nothing else explains his leg. The team debates whether to order a D-dimer or go straight to imaging. Using the Wells criteria, which is the best next step?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","Ankle-brachial index","Contrast ascending venography","D-dimer testing first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer under treatment 1 + calf swelling 4 cm more than the other leg (over 3 cm) 1 + tenderness along the deep veins 1 = 3, a high likelihood of deep vein thrombosis (DVT). In the emergency department, where the score applies, a high-risk patient goes to ultrasound whether or not a D-dimer is drawn. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 3 months ago and has been symptom-free for 6 weeks. His sharp, positional chest pain has now returned with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). C-reactive protein is mildly raised. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Anakinra plus prednisone at once","Prednisone alone for 6 months","Ibuprofen with colchicine, stopped at 2 weeks","Ibuprofen with colchicine for 6 months or more"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His pain returned after 6 weeks symptom-free, a first recurrence. That is treated with nonsteroidal drugs until chest pain resolves and inflammatory markers normalize, plus colchicine for 6 months or more. An interleukin-1 blocker is not needed yet, since his C-reactive protein is only mildly raised and he is not colchicine-resistant. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man with acute pericarditis feels better after 5 days of ibuprofen and colchicine and plans to stop both medicines now. Which counseling point is most important?","choices":["Switch to prednisone to finish faster","Stopping now is safe since the pain is gone","Finish the full course to prevent recurrence","Colchicine can stop; keep the ibuprofen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Feeling better at day 5 is expected, but patient education stresses adherence to the full treatment course to prevent recurrence. That message goes with activity restriction, its rationale and duration, and teaching on red-flag symptoms of recurrence or tamponade. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with visible varicose veins asks what symptoms she is most likely to have. Which should she be told?","choices":["Sharp pain at rest when lying flat","Dull, achy heaviness after standing","Numb, burning feet at night","Calf cramping that eases with rest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Varicose veins can be asymptomatic, but when symptomatic the most common complaint is a dull, achy heaviness or feeling of fatigue brought on by periods of standing. Itching above the ankle or over the veins can also occur, and the veins may be tender. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with a known abdominal aortic aneurysm reports early satiety and pain isolated to his right groin, with no abdominal tenderness. Which explains the groin pain?","choices":["Renal colic from a stone","A femoral pseudoaneurysm","Pressure on the femoral nerve","An inguinal hernia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic, unruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm causes pain with expansion, including isolated groin pain from pressure on the femoral nerve. His early satiety is another listed symptom, and abdominal, back or flank pain may also occur. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A slow sinus rhythm that is causing hypotension and confusion is treated first with which drug?","choices":["Metoprolol","Digoxin","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A slow sinus rhythm causing hypotension and confusion is acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia, and atropine is first line. If it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows; chronic symptomatic bradycardia is treated with a permanent pacemaker instead. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 2-month-old infant with a central line has poor feeding, fast breathing, tachycardia and a changing murmur. Blood cultures are positive and echocardiography shows a vegetation. Which complications are particularly common in infants because of septic emboli?","choices":["Janeway lesions and Roth spots","Glomerulonephritis","Heart failure from valve stenosis","Osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In neonates and infants, septic emboli are common and produce osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia, and neurologic signs such as seizures, hemiparesis and apnea are frequent. This infant's central line, sepsis-like picture and changing murmur fit the infant pattern. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has viridans streptococcal endocarditis diagnosed early, before any heart failure or abscess, and she starts appropriate antibiotics the same day. Which statement about her outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Relatively good prognosis","Valve replacement in most","Highest mortality of any","Death within weeks is likely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her viridans streptococcal endocarditis was diagnosed before any heart failure or abscess, and antibiotics started the same day. That is exactly the condition for a relatively good prognosis: viridans streptococcal disease does relatively well if antibiotic treatment starts before complications occur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation at 170 has a blood pressure of 74/40 and is drowsy and confused. Which is the most appropriate immediate step?","choices":["Oral rate control","Anticoagulate for 21 days","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous rate control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation at 170 with a blood pressure of 74/40, drowsiness and confusion is hemodynamically unstable, a medical emergency. Unstable atrial fibrillation is treated with synchronized cardioversion now, not with rate control or a delayed cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with complete heart block is stabilized with transcutaneous pacing in the emergency department. Which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Implantable defibrillator","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Oral beta blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In complete (third-degree) block no P waves conduct, and the ventricles rely on a slow escape rhythm. Atropine, epinephrine and transcutaneous pacing are the emergent measures that stabilize him; the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive lung disease is admitted with an exacerbation. She is confused, and the monitor shows runs of skipped beats from frequent early, wide QRS complexes without P waves. Her arterial oxygen tension is low. Which underlying problem should be corrected first?","choices":["Her hypoxemia","Her blood glucose","Her blood pressure","Her body temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Early, wide QRS complexes without P waves are premature ventricular contractions, and hypoxemia is a named cause, along with electrolyte imbalance. Her chronic obstructive lung disease exacerbation, confusion and low arterial oxygen tension make hypoxemia the problem to correct first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"In sick sinus syndrome, a fast run that is irregularly irregular with no P waves alternates with pauses. Which rhythm is the fast run?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fast run that is irregularly irregular with no P waves is atrial fibrillation. In tachy-brady syndrome, the form of sick sinus syndrome in which a tachyarrhythmia alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or pauses, atrial fibrillation is the most common cause of the fast rhythm. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with diabetes and known black, shriveled toe tips now has a moist, swollen, blistered forefoot. His temperature is 39.2 C and heart rate 118. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Emergency surgical consultation","Start cilostazol and recheck","Observe until demarcation","Supervised exercise referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A moist, swollen, blistered forefoot with a temperature of 39.2 C and a heart rate of 118 is wet gangrene: bacterial infection, often spore-forming clostridial species, in the affected tissue, with a risk of sepsis. Wet gangrene is a surgical emergency, unlike the dry gangrene of his black, shriveled toe tips. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has been on strict bed rest for six days during an admission for pneumonia and now has swelling and aching of the entire left leg. Which component of the Virchow triad does his prolonged bed rest represent?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Arterial vasospasm","Hypercoagulable state","Venous stasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Six days of strict bed rest is immobilization, which alters blood flow: the venous stasis arm of the Virchow triad. Immobilization carries about a 20-fold increased risk for deep vein thrombosis (DVT), which fits his new swelling and aching of the entire leg. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Compared with Mobitz type I, Mobitz type II block carries a higher risk of which outcome?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Mobitz type I block","Sinus tachycardia","Complete heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II usually originates at the bundle of His, so its escape rhythm is weak and unreliable, and it carries a higher risk of progression to complete (third-degree) block and sudden cardiac death. That is why it is considered dangerous even without symptoms and treated with a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a moderate pericardial effusion was stable yesterday. Today her blood pressure is 96/64, her jugular venous pressure is elevated, and she has new sinus tachycardia at 128. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Immediate pericardiocentesis","Repeat echocardiogram next week","Increase her loop diuretic","Rate control with a beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A known moderate effusion with a new fall in blood pressure to 96/64, an elevated jugular venous pressure and new sinus tachycardia at 128 signals tamponade. Sinus tachycardia may indicate significant hemodynamic compromise and is an indication for immediate pericardiocentesis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a painful ulcer above the medial malleolus with a clean base, fibrinous exudate and serous drainage, surrounded by brown pigmentation and pitting edema. Her pedal pulses are strong and her ankle-brachial index is 1.10. Which measure is essential for the ulcer to heal?","choices":["Topical antibiotic dressings alone","Early surgical valvuloplasty","Oral flavonoid therapy alone","Compression with edema control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A painful ulcer above the medial malleolus with brown pigmentation and pitting edema is a venous ulcer, and venous ulcers will not heal unless edema is controlled and compression is applied. Wound care referral supports healing, which can take 4 to 6 months. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman had a left leg deep vein thrombosis 11 months ago. She now has chronic left leg heaviness, edema and brownish ankle skin that worsen with standing and improve with elevation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Post-thrombotic syndrome","Drug-induced edema","Lymphedema","Recurrent deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Post-thrombotic (postphlebitic) syndrome is symptomatic chronic venous insufficiency that follows a deep vein thrombosis (DVT), from 6 to 24 months after the clot. Her left leg clot 11 months ago, followed by heaviness, edema and brownish ankle skin in that same leg, fits the window and the pattern. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with a femoral pseudoaneurysm after catheterization reports increasing thigh pain and tingling around the swelling. Which explains her pain?","choices":["Critical limb ischemia rest pain","Swelling and nerve compression","Retroperitoneal rupture of the aorta","Atheroemboli to the toes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm presents with pain due to increased pressure from swelling or from nerve compression. Her increasing thigh pain with tingling around the swelling after femoral catheterization matches both mechanisms, the tingling pointing to a compressed nerve. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a story strongly suggesting constrictive pericarditis: edema, ascites, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration). His chest radiograph shows no pericardial calcification. How should this radiograph be interpreted?","choices":["It rules out constrictive disease","It points to restrictive myopathy","It shows the cause is tamponade","It does not exclude constriction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial calcification on a chest radiograph is highly consistent with constriction when present, but most patients do not have it, so a film without calcium cannot rule the diagnosis out. With edema, ascites and the Kussmaul sign, echocardiography is the next step and confirms constrictive physiology in many patients. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has a sudden, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 230 with retrograde (inverted) P waves. An electrocardiogram taken last year in normal sinus rhythm showed a short PR interval and a delta wave (a slurred QRS upstroke). Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Sinus tachycardia","Atrioventricular reentry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pre-excitation, a short PR interval and delta wave, on last year's tracing in normal sinus rhythm points to an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) and the potential for atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia. That rhythm is regular and narrow, above 100 and usually 200 to 300, with hidden or retrograde P waves, matching his rate of 230. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 14, 24, 33, 38"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 months of fever, weight loss and fatigue. A new murmur with a tumor plop (a low-pitched early diastolic sound) is heard, and three sets of blood cultures drawn before any antibiotics are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial myxoma","Lyme disease","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Subacute endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrial myxoma mimics endocarditis with fever and a new murmur, classically a tumor plop or mid-diastolic rumble. Her 2 months of fever and weight loss could suggest endocarditis, but the tumor plop and three negative culture sets drawn before any antibiotics point to this neoplastic mimic. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a heart rate of 122 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. His temperature is 39.2°C and he has a productive cough and crackles at the right base. He is alert with a blood pressure of 126/78. Which is the best treatment for his heart rate?","choices":["Vagal maneuver","Synchronized cardioversion","Treat the infection and fever","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A rate of 122 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS is sinus tachycardia, and with a temperature of 39.2°C, a productive cough and crackles it is an appropriate response to an underlying pathologic process, infection. Appropriate sinus tachycardia is managed by treating that cause, here the infection and fever. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has had six days of worsening left leg pain and coolness. The limb has no sensory loss or weakness, and imaging shows an arterial thrombus. Which intervention fits this time frame?","choices":["Supervised exercise program","Major amputation","Oral aspirin alone","Catheter-directed thrombolysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her ischemia has lasted six days, well within the indication for intra-arterial catheter-directed thrombolysis, which is acute limb ischemia of less than 14 days' duration, with alteplase as the thrombolytic drug. With no sensory loss or weakness, her limb is far from the irreversible category that requires amputation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which cardiac condition is listed among the risk factors for infective endocarditis?","choices":["Stable angina","Sinus bradycardia","Congenital heart disease","White coat hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Congenital heart disease is a listed risk factor for infective endocarditis, along with rheumatic heart disease, degenerative valve disease, intracardiac devices, prosthetic valves and injection drug use. Degenerative valve disease and intracardiac devices are now the leading predisposing conditions in high-income countries. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which definition fits cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Inflammation of the sac lasting under four weeks","Fibrous scarring that leaves the pericardium inelastic","Pericardial fluid under pressure compressing the heart","Extra pericardial fluid with no hemodynamic effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac tamponade is an accumulation of pericardial fluid under pressure, resulting in compression of the heart. Both parts matter: extra fluid alone is a pericardial effusion, and it becomes tamponade only when that fluid is under enough pressure to compress the heart. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with suspected endocarditis has a urinalysis sent as part of her workup. Which urinalysis result is most consistent with endocarditis?","choices":["Glucose and ketones","Bilirubin and urobilinogen","Blood, protein and white cells","Nitrites alone, no cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urinalysis in infective endocarditis can show hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria: blood, protein and white cells. It sits alongside the rest of the workup, which includes three sets of blood cultures, transesophageal echocardiography and a complete blood count showing leukocytosis and a normocytic anemia. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with painful blue toes and palpable pulses asks what the emboli in his toes are made of. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Clot from the left atrium","Fibrin, platelets and cholesterol","Air from a catheter line","Infected valve vegetations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atheroembolism is multiple small deposits of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris embolizing from proximal atherosclerotic lesions. The debris lodges in small vessels of muscle and skin and may not occlude large vessels, which is why his pulses remain palpable despite painful blue toes. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman in stable, rate-controlled atrial flutter of unknown duration is to have elective outpatient cardioversion. Which must come first?","choices":["An adenosine challenge","A permanent pacemaker","21 days of anticoagulation","Catheter ablation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her flutter is stable and rate controlled, and elective outpatient cardioversion of atrial flutter is done after 21 days of anticoagulation. Anticoagulation is part of flutter management in its own right, alongside rate control and ablation as the definitive treatment. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has an electrocardiogram before a sports tryout. The QRS is 0.14 seconds, with an rsR' pattern (a small upward wave, a dip, then a taller second upward wave) in V1 to V3 and a slurred S wave in leads I and aVL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Accessory pathway","Ventricular tachycardia","Left bundle branch block","Right bundle branch block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A QRS over 0.12 seconds (here 0.14), an rsR' pattern ('bunny ears') in V1, V2 and V3 and a slurred S wave in I and aVL are the criteria for right bundle branch block. The right ventricle is activated late from across the septum, and new block can occur without any underlying heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is readmitted 3 weeks after coronary artery bypass surgery with fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). His sternal wound looks clean. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Constrictive pericarditis","Dressler syndrome","Sternal wound infection","Acute graft occlusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fever, pleuritic pain eased by sitting forward and a friction rub 3 weeks after coronary artery bypass surgery, with a clean sternal wound, point to post-cardiotomy pericarditis. Pericarditis after myocardial infarction or after cardiac surgery is Dressler syndrome, a listed cause of pericardial disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy has fatigue and exercise intolerance along with leg edema, hepatomegaly and ascites, but her lungs are relatively clear. Which pattern of heart failure is expected to dominate in this condition?","choices":["Isolated left-sided failure","Right-sided heart failure","Pure systolic failure","High-output heart failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her leg edema, hepatomegaly and ascites with relatively clear lungs are the right-sided pattern. In restrictive cardiomyopathy, filling pressures are elevated in both ventricles, but right heart failure may dominate over left, with an elevated jugular venous pressure, bilateral pedal edema and ascites. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with a prosthetic valve and a past episode of anaphylaxis to penicillin needs a dental cleaning and can take pills. Which prophylactic agent should be avoided?","choices":["Azithromycin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline","Clarithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She had anaphylaxis to penicillin, and cephalexin (like cefazolin and ceftriaxone) should not be used in patients with a history of anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria to penicillin or ampicillin. Her prosthetic valve still calls for prophylaxis, so an oral macrolide or doxycycline is used instead. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with newly diagnosed deep vein thrombosis has chronic kidney disease with a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Admission for intravenous heparin","Adjusting heparin by lab results","Overlapping heparin with warfarin","Beginning therapy as an outpatient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min, she needs to be hospitalized to start treatment, so beginning therapy at home is the approach to avoid. In hospital she receives unfractionated heparin (UFH) as an intravenous bolus followed by a continuous infusion, adjusted by laboratory results and overlapped with warfarin. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man in ventricular fibrillation arrest is shocked several times, and his rhythm changes to a flat line with no electrical activity in two leads. What does this change mean for his outlook?","choices":["No change in prognosis","A much worse prognosis","Successful defibrillation","A better chance of survival"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A flat line with no electrical activity is asystole. Ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation have the best prognosis of the arrest rhythms, while asystole is unshockable, carries a poor prognosis and is usually an end-of-life rhythm, so his outlook has worsened; compressions and epinephrine continue. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What most commonly causes a lower extremity arteriovenous fistula?","choices":["Blunt trauma to the thigh","Groin access for a procedure","Surgical creation for dialysis","Congenital malformation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lower extremity arteriovenous (AV) fistulas are most commonly iatrogenic, caused by groin access for endovascular procedures. Upper extremity fistulas, by contrast, are most commonly created on purpose for hemodialysis access. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 182 with palpitations and mild anxiety. Her blood pressure is 128/80, oxygen saturation 98%, and she is alert without chest pain. Which treatment should be tried first?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow complex tachycardia at 182 with a blood pressure of 128/80, normal oxygen saturation and an alert patient without chest pain is stable supraventricular tachycardia. A vagal maneuver is first line, then adenosine (repeated once if needed), with synchronized cardioversion kept for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
